WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.270
A tool loop alternates between a model decision and application execution.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.816
Each iteration consumes time, tokens, and possibly a scarce downstream resource.

00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:14.716
Define a maximum number of model calls, a deadline, input and output

00:00:14.716 --> 00:00:17.091
limits, and a successful stopping condition.

00:00:17.241 --> 00:00:20.557
The lab allows at most five model calls in Main and rejects

00:00:20.557 --> 00:00:22.768
an unfinished loop when its budget is exhausted.

00:00:22.918 --> 00:00:26.398
A deadline check between steps does not interrupt a blocked call.

00:00:26.548 --> 00:00:31.480
Network adapters need their own connection and response timeouts, and tool implementations

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.124
must avoid unbounded blocking.

00:00:33.274 --> 00:00:36.940
A thread interrupt is cooperative, not a universal kill switch.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.310
Bound the work at every dependency boundary and distinguish a partial result

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:42.366
from completed success.

00:00:42.516 --> 00:00:45.473
A defective model may request lookupOrder forever.

00:00:45.623 --> 00:00:49.742
AgentTest runs this behavior and expects a budget-exhausted exception.

00:00:49.892 --> 00:00:53.982
A tool may return too much text; the engine rejects it rather

00:00:53.982 --> 00:00:55.345
than growing history indefinitely.

00:00:55.495 --> 00:01:00.200
The current HTTP adapter checks response size after buffering, so a production

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.728
ingress limit or streaming cap remains a hardening task.

00:01:03.878 --> 00:01:06.680
Make a model return the same tool request forever.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.848
Then implement a repeated-action detector and compare it with the absolute call

00:01:10.848 --> 00:01:11.183
limit.

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:13.315
Explain why both may be useful.

00:01:13.465 --> 00:01:16.647
The call limit guarantees a finite number of decisions.

00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:22.061
A repeated-action detector can stop waste earlier, but identical calls may legitimately

00:01:22.061 --> 00:01:23.377
produce changed results.

00:01:23.527 --> 00:01:27.403
Include arguments and relevant state in the detector and retain the absolute

00:01:27.403 --> 00:01:29.341
budget as the reliable outer bound.

00:01:29.491 --> 00:01:30.162
Question.

00:01:30.312 --> 00:01:32.721
Does a circuit breaker replace a deadline?

00:01:32.872 --> 00:01:33.448
Answer.

00:01:33.598 --> 00:01:34.082
No.

00:01:34.232 --> 00:01:38.859
A breaker controls admission after failures; each admitted call still needs a

00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:39.244
timeout.

00:01:39.394 --> 00:01:40.065
Question.

00:01:40.215 --> 00:01:42.563
What is a correct budget-exhausted result?

00:01:42.713 --> 00:01:43.290
Answer.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.881
A controlled incomplete outcome with useful available evidence, not a fabricated successful

00:01:48.881 --> 00:01:49.335
answer.
